  "ism_aggregation": "the ISM profile's mean over each token's six positions. IG attributes to tokens and ISM substitutes bases, so the comparison is made at the coarser of the two resolutions; folding IG's one value onto six bases instead would invent a per-base number ADR 0007 forbids.",
  "variants": {
    "magnitude": "|attribution| against the ISM position profile (mean |delta| over a position's three alternative bases), both aggregated to the five tokens. Answers whether the two techniques rank the tokens alike.",
    "signed": "the signed attribution against the signed ISM delta, mean over a position's three alternative bases, both aggregated to the five tokens. Answers whether they agree about which way a token pushes the prediction. A head can rank alike and sign oppositely, so the two variants are reported apart and neither replaces the other."
  },
  "agreement_sign": {
    "magnitude": 1,
    "signed": -1
  },
  "signed_convention_note": "In the `signed` variant, agreement is a **negative** correlation and `per_row.share_negative` is the share of rows that agree. IG's sign is the contribution of the base that is present; an ISM delta's sign is the effect of substituting it away, so the two run opposite by construction on a token the model relies on. `agreement_sign` carries the expected direction per variant; multiply by it to read a correlation as agreement. The `magnitude` variant has no such convention - there, agreement is positive.",
  "profile_definition": "mean |ISM delta| over a position's three alternative bases, per row, position and head; the reference base's slot is a true zero and is excluded from the mean",
  "mismatch_note": "A low correlation is a limit of token-level attribution, not a bug. The last token binds the PAM (24-26) to the right flank (27-29), so base-resolution ISM and token-resolution IG have no reason to coincide there (docs/adr/0007-ism-carries-pam-resolution-not-ig.md). Disagreement is reported as measured; nothing is retried, re-thresholded or dropped to raise it, and neither technique is treated as the other's ground truth.",
  "population": {
    "name": "ig_subset",
    "rows": 400,
    "population_digest": "68c3702ce4c811e1dc4e5a5bc897324575e27a6deef902cefd6bee9becfce9a4",
    "swept_rows": 3851,
    "note": "The correlation is reported on the frozen IG subset, which is the smaller of the two populations: ISM swept the whole analysis intersection and IG ran on the subset. `swept_rows` is the sweep's population and is not this report's denominator."
  },
  "pooling": "forbidden",
  "pooling_note": "One entry per stratum and no combined one. The two strata answer different questions over differently-shaped populations: generalisation is sequence generalisation on unseen protospacers, pam_library is a PAM experiment on 5 targets, so adding their row counts would add a sample size to a replication factor. `population` is not their sum either: the intersection also holds rows that are in **neither** stratum (non-library, non-NGG), which is why it is reported under its own name rather than as a total.",
  "nan_policy_note": "A correlation over five tokens is undefined, not zero, where a profile is constant; such rows are counted as `undefined_rows` and a single undefined number is written as null.",
  "rollout_excluded": "Attention rollout is not an input. It has no head axis at all (docs/adr/0008-attention-rollout-is-head-invariant.md), so it cannot describe a per-head reading, and every figure here is per head.",
  "claim_limit": "Every figure here describes the model's internal information use - which positions or tokens its prediction depends on, and how its heads order one row. None of it is a claim about physical DNA/protein interaction.",
